def lps(s):
    n = len(s)
    dp = [[0 for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]

    # Every single character is a palindrome of length 1
    for i in range(n):
        dp[i][i] = 1

    # Fill the table
    for length in range(2, n + 1):
        for start in range(n - length + 1):
            end = start + length - 1
            if s[start] == s[end]:
                dp[start][end] = dp[start + 1][end - 1] + 2
            else:
                dp[start][end] = max(dp[start + 1][end], dp[start][end - 1])

    return dp[0][n - 1]
```

The `lps` function initializes a 2D list `dp` where `dp[i][j]` represents the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the substring `s[i:j+1]`. It then iterates over all possible lengths of substrings starting from 2 up to the full length of the string. For each substring, it checks if the characters at the start and end indices are the same. If they are, it adds 2 to the length of the longest palindromic subsequence found in the substring without these two characters. Otherwise, it takes the maximum length of the palindromes found in the substrings that exclude either the start or the end character. Finally, it returns the value in `dp[0][n-1]`, which represents the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the entire string.

